Z||000000||RossT||11-11-2001||08:11 PM||||I am in the process of changing my fuel pump. The previous onwner, a month ago, replaced the pump with a carter, and it was humming way to loud.  I got everything apart and am ready to put a new AC Delco pump on, but I want to check to see if the fuel strainer that was on the carter is an AC Delco or after market.  Here is a pick.  Can you tell if this is a new GM strainer or not?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image027_4.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image031_4.jpg"> <P>If not a gm strainer, does it really matter?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroHarry||11-11-2001||08:17 PM||||My friend CaptFiero is converting his to a 3800SC.<BR>He had just pruchased a new one and yes it looked like a tampon.... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||jelly2m8||11-11-2001||08:34 PM||||Thats not an original GM strainer, those are more of a cylinder shape, and longer.<P> The one in the picture will function just as well as an original one though||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||11-12-2001||01:11 AM||||Ross,<P>What you have is an aftermarket strainer.  They work just as well as the Delco ones, so don't worry about it.  It's the fuel pumps themselves that need to be genuine AC Delco, and you have that covered already.||||reg||1
